{"name":"Daredevil","hex":"#ab4343","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"daredevil","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person who engages in very risky behavior, especially one who is motivated by a craving for excitement or attention. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Recklessly bold; adventurous."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/daredevil"}],"entries":[{"title":"Daredevil (Lev Gleason Publications)","blocks":[{"text":"Daredevil, a superhero created by Jack Binder, starred in comics from Lev Gleason Publications during the 1930s–1940s period that historians and fans call the Golden Age of comic books. The character was retroactively established into the Image Universe by Image Comics in the 1990s as its first character. The character is unrelated to Marvel Comics' Daredevil, and recent renditions of the character have often renamed him Doubledare or The Death-Defying Devil to avoid confusion and potential lawsuits."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/DDGoldenAge5.jpg/330px-DDGoldenAge5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daredevil_(Lev_Gleason_Publications)"},{"title":"Daredevil (Marvel Comics character)","blocks":[{"text":"Daredevil is a superhero appearing in American comic books published by Marvel Comics. Created by writer-editor Stan Lee and artist Bill Everett, with some input from Jack Kirby, the character first appeared in Daredevil #1."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/14/Daredevil_65.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daredevil_(Marvel_Comics_character)"},{"title":"Daredevil (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Daredevil is a 2003 American superhero film written and directed by Mark Steven Johnson, based on the Marvel Comics character\nof the same name created by Stan Lee and Bill Everett. The film stars Ben Affleck as Matt Murdock, a blind lawyer who fights for justice in the courtroom and on the streets of New York as the masked vigilante Daredevil. Jennifer Garner, Michael Clarke Duncan, Colin Farrell, Joe Pantoliano, Jon Favreau, and David Keith also star in supporting roles."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/0/04/Daredevil_poster.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daredevil_(film)"},{"title":"Daredevil (TV series)","blocks":[{"text":"Marvel's Daredevil is an American television series created by Drew Goddard for the streaming service Netflix, based on the Marvel Comics character Daredevil. It is set in the Marvel Cinematic Universe (MCU), sharing continuity with the films of the franchise, and was the first Marvel Netflix series leading to the crossover miniseries The Defenders (2017). Daredevil was produced by Marvel Television in association with ABC Studios. Steven S. DeKnight served as showrunner for the first season, with Doug Petrie and Marco Ramirez taking over as co-showrunners for the second, and Erik Oleson joining the series as showrunner for the third; Goddard served as a consultant for the series."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/8/8a/Daredevil_%28TV_series%29_logo.jpg/330px-Daredevil_%28TV_series%29_logo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daredevil_(TV_series)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Daredevil_Battles_Hitler_cover_-_number_1.jpg/500px-Daredevil_Battles_Hitler_cover_-_number_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Daredevil_Battles_Hitler_cover_-_number_1.jpg/500px-Daredevil_Battles_Hitler_cover_-_number_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Daredevil Battles Hitler (July 1941), the premiere issue of Daredevil Comics; art by Charles Biro and Bob Wood.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADaredevil_Battles_Hitler_cover_-_number_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/f/fd/Daredevil_splashpage_-_number_1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/f/fd/Daredevil_splashpage_-_number_1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Splash page of the first issue of Daredevil (April 1964) features the hero in his original costume. Art by Jack Kirby (penciler) and Bill Everett (inker).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADaredevil_splashpage_-_number_1.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/a/a9/Daredevil_cover_-_number_184.jpeg/250px-Daredevil_cover_-_number_184.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/a9/Daredevil_cover_-_number_184.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/a/a9/Daredevil_cover_-_number_184.jpeg/250px-Daredevil_cover_-_number_184.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cover of Daredevil #184 (July 1982). Art by Frank Miller and Klaus Janson.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADaredevil_cover_-_number_184.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/6.20.19JohnRomitaJrByLuigiNovi27.jpg/500px-6.20.19JohnRomitaJrByLuigiNovi27.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/6.20.19JohnRomitaJrByLuigiNovi27.jpg/1280px-6.20.19JohnRomitaJrByLuigiNovi27.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm